{"Ask a question worth answering": "f66b527a5242241b", "Orientation, readiness & business-question framing": "8571141d5c9aa812", "M00 / M01": "e9f7ef7ee9036c16", "Question framer": "eb1fbf111cef09ab", "Turn a broad concern into an evidence question, define what each row represents, and make the next decision explicit.": "fb9b27dadf568b47", "Name the stakeholder, decision, population, and time horizon.": "d98750c074b529df", "Distinguish the business problem from the software task.": "bc2174471b9f2ba6", "Check grain, units, provenance, and tool readiness.": "4da187321510be10", "Start with the decision": "edeabf5d8be43b79", "Concern → stakeholder → action → evidence": "9fb1794808230d89", "A business problem describes a condition. A decision names a choice. An analytic question identifies evidence that could inform that choice.": "39874e9dd869eb88", "For a fictional museum café, ask whether next month's weekend staffing should change, then identify which service measure could inform that choice.": "978af7dd61f53737", "Define the evidence": "3de7f159b681306c", "Population → grain → variable → provenance": "69c1b64a112629e7", "Grain states what one row represents. A customer may have several transactions; transaction counts are not customer counts.": "edc32023b378c5ac", "A row for each café visit supports a visit-level wait measure. Line-item rows must be reconciled before counting visits.": "3d5c7226a6e5a86c", "Set a measure and a guardrail": "b9dde95026a33d72", "Numerator / eligible denominator → unit → target": "97a088bc1a7c5882", "A KPI needs an operational definition, reporting period, eligible population, and denominator. A guardrail limits harm or unacceptable data quality.": "6f002e038229a61e", "Pair a service target with a missing-wait guardrail so a good rate cannot hide poor records.": "ca91226e0bee0f50", "Make reasoning inspectable": "f8ec70bff2e0b6d1", "Observation → inference → assumption → next action": "bd5aa608e561eca4", "Software calculates. People judge whether evidence supports a recommendation. Keep observed facts separate from claims about causes or the future.": "494e988a0f43d2e1", "Report what the available visits show, explain uncertainty, and propose a bounded check before a permanent decision.": "d74f6a2beaf4f7d5", "Which is an analytic question?": "52976ca13c54f528", "Open SPSS.": "d907fd64525f413c", "What share of eligible visits exceeded ten minutes last month?": "2cb70b8eb7b58c1b", "Service feels slow.": "8cd5b1908eeb6afb", "That names a software task. First identify the evidence needed.": "8863a7506fd56c6d", "Correct. It identifies a measurable outcome, eligible cases, and a period.": "3e7b966952d6896b", "That describes a concern. It still needs an outcome and scope.": "57b213588cfb23d0", "One customer makes three visits. At visit grain, how many observations are there?": "ac71a5008fd07621", "One": "8b12507783d5beca", "Three": "926f52d1c1e19c0c", "It depends on the chart.": "dcb1b42c7990b7e0", "One is the customer count, not the visit count.": "517db903998f6bef", "Correct. Grain determines the unit being counted.": "3fc65bfea843dca3", "A chart does not change the observational unit.": "431297bd163c47a6", "What makes a recommendation easier to audit?": "49e6ac25b331d9b4", "Separate observations, assumptions, and the proposed action.": "c2b089b177226b63", "Use the most advanced software.": "8263ec360ee9ab94", "Remove every limitation.": "4d6b4cc8769f243d", "Correct. A visible reasoning path lets another person inspect the decision.": "5fcb9bb1d6d3cb17", "Complex software cannot replace clear reasoning.": "8136b99d19fc5b5a", "Limits matter. Removing them makes evidence harder to judge.": "73b54aef6aacaee7", "Give every measure a contract": "d30092d7d505091e", "KPIs, variables, grain & data stories": "e79c77510bcd287c", "M01 / M02": "e129b2d5e1c830cd", "Measure designer": "adcc8a0e2ad616bf", "Choose a valid measure, keep denominators visible, and match the display to the variable and question.": "3788727a82033c6b", "Identify nominal, ordinal, interval, and ratio measures.": "2450b724d4f7f519", "Define a KPI's population, unit, denominator, and period.": "e326c27d03dd9ea2", "Critique chart scale, aggregation, and the implied story.": "950e12a484c56586", "Decompose the measure": "3e846c154b255a87", "Construct → operational variable → values": "fdcea3eeaf102c5b", "A construct such as satisfaction becomes an observed variable through a stated question or measurement process. Coding does not automatically establish equal intervals.": "2cb490a57bafb00d", "A single satisfaction item is ordered categorical. Dollar revenue has a meaningful zero and supports ratios.": "192cc3f1e29b0cf0", "Choose the right unit": "57f4dcd55a319eae", "Grain → key → population → period": "08af50646c1049ce", "Keep one observation at the intended grain. Define eligibility and dates before aggregating. A line-item join can duplicate an order-level outcome.": "b200c6e53b959e1b", "An order with three products is still one order for an order-level late-delivery rate.": "ff967d35d355a0c6", "Show the denominator": "b9389b5251a84e5d", "Known outcomes → missing outcomes → bounds": "6499a9166f54546f", "The rate among measured cases answers a different question from the rate among all eligible cases. Unknown outcomes cannot simply be counted as successes.": "43cc093a51637900", "Show the observed rate and plausible best/worst bounds when outcomes are missing.": "5abece9045c5068e", "Tell a bounded data story": "025268efc6d135e8", "Question → display → interpretation → action": "59900202c15e5914", "Use bars for category comparisons, histograms for numeric distributions, and scatterplots for two numeric variables. Labels, units, source, and scale support honest comparison.": "ea099dc6752558b2", "Start count bars at zero. Joining unordered categories with a line implies an unsupported ordering.": "5aa4fb9b325d4625", "A single five-category satisfaction item is usually what level?": "1d774f5d02dffc9b", "Nominal": "1b9ffa43806608e6", "Ordinal": "6f266bc53af518cc", "Ratio": "1348beb03ed9067b", "The categories have an order, beyond nominal labels.": "be17cdbe44d5a744", "Correct. Order is meaningful; coding 1–5 does not establish equal gaps.": "064cde2cbacb263b", "There is no meaningful absolute zero or justified claim that 4 is twice 2.": "50ee3045bad71292", "A wait outcome is missing. What should you do?": "f0a2b2126e09eb4b", "Treat it as zero minutes.": "e6b1af0f38bb8fe5", "State missingness and denominator, then inspect its cause.": "97a949fe023190e9", "Exclude it without mentioning it.": "6852cf74cd492f6e", "Zero is a valid measurement, not a missing-value code.": "f2504f8f75505bc0", "Correct. The missing mechanism can change KPI interpretation.": "34f04319f84c7b4f", "That changes the population summarized and conceals uncertainty.": "90a4975e4b30189d", "Which chart shows a numeric wait-time distribution?": "0da85f46f67f5a72", "Histogram": "b32dc489fba87a9a", "Pie chart": "9899e8baa953e351", "A line connecting customer IDs": "b1d9dd7cadf4c9ca", "Correct. Bins summarize counts over numeric ranges.": "1ecafc5ea67b1c92", "A pie compares parts of a whole, not numeric shape.": "6699bff8897f2138", "Identifiers are labels. A joining line adds an unsupported order.": "4b26477294260b42", "Read the shape of the evidence": "2458559fec511768", "Data quality, distributions & descriptive evidence": "755f833b654777b8", "M02": "03864c101b6cb403", "Distribution detective": "bfab92749f30a4cc", "Audit records, compare center and spread, and explore how unusual observations change a summary.": "abc1ed65d0cac210", "Inspect duplicates, missingness, and unusual values before summarizing.": "c7eff6c0f4fb4a41", "Compare mean, median, standard deviation, quartiles, and IQR.": "86d768535dd56844", "Choose useful displays and explain descriptive limits.": "c056d207f747c78d", "Audit before averaging": "979a555300261108", "Raw rows → distinct cases → valid measurements": "7d0f166857d2f133", "A duplicated key may be a repeated export or a key problem. Investigate it. Missing and unusual observations need separate treatment.": "7226582d69af62e8", "Keep a raw copy and reconciliation record. Report eligible cases and valid measurements.": "bc1db74aa3ea59c7", "Compare center": "3b0d021d0b0ad1f2", "Mean ↔ median ↔ shape": "33ca54a8d0dfdbff", "The mean uses every magnitude. The median uses order and position. A long right tail can pull the mean above the median.": "f12962178d6a609f", "Five delivery times can retain the same median while one long delivery substantially increases the mean.": "b4551e76bc9a6135", "Describe spread": "ad276d7b7522f1e9", "SD ↔ range ↔ quartiles ↔ IQR": "1d1501fc6ac80ca8", "Standard deviation summarizes variation around the mean. IQR is Q3 minus Q1. An outlier fence is a review flag, not proof of error.": "ee9a0091b486ff20", "Check a flagged value against its source. Preserve valid unusual observations and report sensitivity if it matters.": "48b7513aeddd948f", "Choose the display": "a0f66b2263da5853", "Distribution → comparison → relationship": "411273160019ff53", "A dot plot shows each small-sample observation. A histogram depends on bins. Box plots compactly compare distributions but do not show every shape feature.": "cada1b048d2ea933", "Show group sizes and missingness. A descriptive difference alone does not establish causation.": "2eadaf85c72abf87", "One valid delivery time rises from 26 to 80 minutes. Which changes more?": "5616ff975d39661a", "The median": "afedc2fa61a7c1ad", "The mean": "a5b126a4b46553e1", "Neither changes": "5a173fe9337d4533", "The middle sorted value stays 22 minutes.": "e86bedffc0a5d6b7", "Correct. The mean uses the changed magnitude; the middle value remains 22.": "152d2f35812f82a9", "The sum increases, so the mean increases.": "5d503402ac44279a", "An observation lies beyond an IQR fence. What does that establish?": "a9eeaa9e4c0cd9c5", "It is a data error.": "330f2c3e0f5a7abb", "It should be investigated.": "7fd89008646c6740", "It must be replaced by the mean.": "59802753358da58e", "An unusual value may be real. A flag is not proof of error.": "119fc0340cbc0370", "Correct. Check provenance and plausibility, then explain any treatment.": "a5b385b818c8d4a0", "Replacement requires a defensible reason, beyond a flag.": "d5204f28a66bbcdf", "Group A has a larger average than Group B in an extract. Can you infer causation?": "33753791a66cc3c1", "Yes, the average proves it.": "915a27897a5f5deb", "Only if the bars are separated.": "faee281ca16420b0", "No. Design, selection, and other differences still matter.": "d641f55b19e9c39a", "An average is descriptive. Study design governs causal interpretation.": "faa1c5d77560c2c6", "Visual separation does not resolve confounding or selection.": "096cb6f0f1b6ad45", "Correct. State the observed difference and design limits.": "50446ee5bcb98c19", "Make decisions under uncertainty": "38e91efeddf609d0", "Conditional risk, expected value & simulation": "28f78ec825e9f3a4", "M03": "a678755b344c536e", "Risk navigator": "63df2e707126f10c", "Distinguish joint from conditional probability, compare payoffs across states, and inspect risk alongside averages.": "9d7a2ac855631da5", "Calculate conditional probability with the correct reference group.": "d6c7939ba2181a0f", "Compare actions using expected value and downside.": "a3492f4d4cb55bb6", "Use simulation as a model-based experiment and test assumptions.": "4b1864ef591a0e57", "Name the reference group": "f15cedf3a0b33e79", "Event A and B → P(A | B)": "73cfcc7af0cbb9f1", "Conditional probability restricts the denominator to the condition. P(A | B) need not equal P(B | A). Base rates matter.": "41d466700b5c0de1", "A screen can detect most true cases while many positive screens are false alarms when the event is rare.": "b3140d4f39ccd557", "Choose across uncertain states": "e028de42af199ea6", "States → probabilities → payoffs → expected value": "5c32b3a7b451e410", "Expected value weights outcomes by probability. Probabilities sum to one, and payoffs use compatible units and horizons.": "ad6154756c204ca7", "Higher expected profit may come with a larger adverse-state loss. Compare both.": "e59d51e6f42467a9", "Model and simulate": "b0ea615e2c602ebe", "Inputs → trial logic → outputs → sensitivity": "f4110d13942fb2a8", "Simulation explores assumptions. Its output is an estimate, not population truth. Inspect reproducibility and trial counts.": "922aab3966897bfd", "Separate an analytic expectation from a particular run's mean. Check units, boundary inputs, and downside.": "214a9adcd7cb9877", "Communicate bounded risk": "bababe99b4c9a6a1", "Expected result + downside + reversal condition": "8314aa2898bc3b43", "Explain which assumption could reverse the action. A fifth percentile is a cut-off with roughly 5% below it, not a guaranteed minimum.": "ab498f62b0522e9d", "Name the threshold, loss risk, and next evidence before committing resources.": "2581a2d3ec25f509", "For P(event | positive screen), what is the denominator?": "c0d95b8c962c3c02", "All true events": "768473c7241c3aab", "All positive screens": "9de89b241449908a", "The entire population": "1cbdbf257e1fd9fd", "That denominator belongs to sensitivity, P(positive | event).": "e91c1456c08815d1", "Correct. Conditioning on a positive screen restricts the reference group.": "94a2325667dde7b5", "The whole population is used for a joint proportion, not this conditional probability.": "c9655f6fa0d6b5ba", "Does higher expected profit guarantee higher profit next time?": "54a061a336aae275", "Yes": "85a39ab345d672ff", "No": "1ea442a134b2a184", "Only with a large chart": "fd75ffc2ba2f8705", "Expected value averages across states. One realized state can be worse.": "32533f2df68988a3", "Correct. Check downside, assumptions, and decision horizon as well.": "b4323db257a4fcb3", "Display size does not change uncertainty.": "47d9034237b477c3", "What does the fifth percentile of profit mean?": "f031382541e1d047", "A guaranteed profit floor": "125cd6b2a13500c7", "About 5% of modeled outcomes fall below that cut-off": "ae84353df6bd2dd0", "The average profit": "c1a0be0facc954f2", "Some outcomes are worse. It is not a floor.": "654cfeff8a7e36df", "Correct. Describe the model and horizon as well as the percentile.": "548087a3b7c472ae", "A percentile describes position, not the mean.": "9a981caf7a504df7", "Put uncertainty around the estimate": "10417c6c733b2003", "Sampling, bias, confidence intervals & precision": "58bd95927503271b", "M04": "55b69f4a0862f7f1", "Uncertainty interpreter": "677f48f9e10141a4", "Separate individual variability from estimate variability, audit sampling, and interpret confidence correctly.": "5459cfef79fc2ca5", "Distinguish population, frame, sample, parameter, and statistic.": "464fb8182a66eae2", "Explain standard error and the square-root rule.": "5c82788c34511a5d", "Interpret confidence and precision while checking bias and independence.": "6cf85f6117662afb", "Audit the sample": "615dde194df16c31", "Population → frame → draw → response": "dd8ef3b785c915de", "A probability draw does not ensure unbiased responses. Coverage, nonresponse, and clustering can weaken generalization.": "287ba8007e197b75", "Record who was eligible, reachable, selected, and observed. More responses from the same selection mechanism may preserve bias.": "497c195545220de5", "Separate two variations": "c6fdee8c7c423d3b", "Individual SD → sampling distribution → SE": "c1198b19f782fcea", "SD describes observations. Standard error describes estimator variability across repeated samples. For an independent mean, SE is approximately s / √n.": "d050273702fb6dfb", "Quadrupling n approximately halves SE when design and population variation remain comparable.": "952c18b0d6c2fd27", "Build and read an interval": "fd463da8b2dbbefc", "Estimate ± critical value × SE": "88bbef379166c2d6", "For a mean with unknown population SD, use the appropriate Student t critical value under the procedure's conditions. A 95% method has approximately 95% long-run coverage.": "9e7f132050c16f67", "It does not contain 95% of individual customers. A calculated fixed interval either covers the fixed population mean or it does not.": "cc0a29104d8390dd", "Plan useful precision": "ea8d9e9a98fbb97a", "Margin of error → sample-size plan → bias check": "d1f93197fd0153c7", "Precision depends on confidence, variability, sample size, and design. Narrow intervals can still miss the target in a biased sample.": "6e3ee14a1c91fdc3", "Specify an actionable margin of error, round sample-size requirements upward, and check the sampling frame.": "43084eca7cfa24d7", "What does a 95% confidence procedure describe?": "b55827524aa1e9b3", "95% of individual outcomes lie in each interval.": "4a4e264266f69e80", "About 95% of repeated comparable intervals cover the fixed parameter.": "d58d0c4c0b397680", "There is always a 95% chance the sample mean is correct.": "505ecdee87867970", "That concerns individual outcomes, not uncertainty around a population estimate.": "93fdb0415f6cb2f8", "Correct. Confidence describes long-run coverage under the assumptions.": "af44b366bee3b57a", "The sample mean is an estimate. Coverage concerns the population parameter and procedure.": "f1e0612f2ae28458", "If n rises from 25 to 100 under the same independent design, what happens to SE?": "5b3dda5153c08ce3", "It roughly halves.": "7165ab9797d07d20", "It doubles.": "404e26e049616898", "It remains unchanged.": "005271a7e6d1009f", "Correct. √100 is twice √25, so the denominator doubles.": "7c2ddbfee0a021d2", "More independent observations reduce estimate variability here.": "51d6711523b8f0de", "SD may remain similar, but standard error decreases.": "8bb14eb29495aede", "Can a much larger convenience sample automatically repair selection bias?": "10775453fb782efb", "Only at 99% confidence": "c8e48f4e4639d89b", "The selection mechanism remains. A precise estimate can still be biased.": "5ec6f31a4168ee62", "Correct. Fix sampling and investigate coverage and nonresponse.": "174365138afa8538", "Higher confidence changes width, not selection.": "335d3e8ac9cf3107", "Hypothesis tests, effect size & A/B decisions": "2e73053f36a07bb3", "Midterm Decision Analysis": "e35da9dc100742ac", "Group comparisons & test selection": "b1ef82981f596993", "Correlation, categorical relationships & confounding": "6f347dd4b93208aa", "Regression & conditional interpretation": "b52a56d7554319c4", "Diagnostics, forecasting & fairness": "61c0b6a6252c19a2", "Project revision & reproducibility": "2f6a0b676df6d1a6", "Executive communication & rehearsal": "3fbff6389af5a99f", "Final showcase & oral defense": "b2eb82fbd431cafe", "Mean": "727a7d114f4d518e", "Median": "daab7c435134742a", "Sample SD": "26e4813afa131241", "Standard error": "564347aa28153b53", "Margin of error": "47887666f49d2583", "This run covers": "d51e2fd993c1618d", "Observed late rate": "cceb5637bbbd7c37", "All-case lower bound": "a40bb183adc759a0", "All-case upper bound": "a3a650dc3e4f75b4", "P(event | positive)": "38dc6246801b6a24", "Sensitivity": "ebb57a260d58207c", "Large expected profit": "4d2ff9a14c48c3cf", "Flexible expected profit": "3285a7414b65deea", "Display": "34e108c0896d0158", "Histogram bin width": "fac66e6b2334ef78", "Individual observations · dot plot": "b7ca9576b78fc016", "Distribution · histogram": "17b45678c1080e9b", "10 minutes": "b075b6e113fcf02a", "20 minutes": "cd04501cb57e64ba", "Last delivery time:": "d805dc8d31221306", "Selection bias in sample mean:": "a9f5b55b195fc21c", "Probability of rain:": "a4c353786fe7a974", "Underlying event rate:": "e095c7ca403cc66f", "Independent observations per sample": "8e50e2efaeb640d6", "Confidence level": "679326f4a3dc10aa", "Download these five values · CSV": "d690c7f5ecd94f37", "Draw another 100 samples": "9b3dc2d0f4c5ec0f", "Pause & predict": "337505c81a4e814d", "Choose a prediction, then check its explanation.": "dfeb6c814f7ed6a0", "All three checks & evidence stamp →": "e03a5eff9f21c957", "One long delivery. Two different summaries.": "03fe8d3105e7b2d3", "Which summary follows the tail?": "d723b680880ff6d3", "Can a narrow interval still be wrong?": "7533cb77d42eb00d", "Conditional risk needs a reference group": "3d66c6b8d7db8e3f", "Confidence is a property of the procedure": "8373a7bf6574e20c", "Screen outcomes among 1,000 products": "697aeeb4df0ad204", "Actual condition": "42bb3213a43bfe62", "Positive screen": "7c39310b4f558222", "Negative screen": "a1c2b9bf065e28d2", "Event": "4e1f49a9c8ae8a15", "No event": "99e875089b875b58", "Question": "289aff12b04274cb", "Measures": "aabaa8ff445c09b9", "Distribution": "72a23e549d099bbd", "Risk": "0711a8d636e4c068", "Uncertainty": "77b9ebf987ea64c6", "Knowledge checks": "adb1ba1a43f60d51", "Correct": "aca01a1a3a27d35c", "Not yet": "a9549d7d06368d83", "Build the question": "f1d9ec023711a13b", "Your decision question": "043b097b74cc3a47", "Stakeholder": "be4882074a25a567", "Decision": "640ae4baf96061fe", "Population": "5b828861aec15d60", "Measure": "ebf940f2b3eaa86c", "Period": "6e795d4d3cc21c5c", "The denominator changes the story": "3679e309dff038c8", "The ingredient list for a useful question": "e70be8c1cc73c601", "Complete all five ingredients to build the question.": "ff8da64f7d2e6bf7", "Claim this evidence stamp": "9f5fb3d515a4f876", "Evidence stamp earned": "f27924899d4a61c5", "Explore the experiment and answer all three checks correctly.": "fe36dc00b790bc72", "Explore the experiment first.": "79c7691d5e350543", "All three checks must be correct before claiming this stamp.": "d11c0eebb005952e", "use strict": "d96cdf6b2af72caf", "Museum café manager": "6382e07c23a57980", "adjust weekend staffing": "92656cccaccd1894", "eligible weekend café visits": "00b519ec9718d055", "share waiting over ten minutes": "45a35af16791276f", "next month": "9e15dcbeb49a6a1c", "\" type=\"range\" min=\"": "a1a7de68717f6880", "\" max=\"": "90d299af5b172e37", "\" step=\"": "393aa7abe00956d5", "\" value=\"": "f65563229a300311", "Choose a valid session, question, and option.": "ec7665bf943a55aa", "· evidence checks": "bf4b7d2e0ed012a6", "Explain what you found.": "48d7f1566ae80d2b", "Wrong answers are invitations to rethink. Read the feedback and try again. These checks use independent practice examples.": "06fc1fbde866ecd6", "Earn the": "28520dc560d7153c", "Stamp earned ✓": "e67ff05a3c6b5f74", "Earn the {badge} stamp": "06af57cee0f2a6e3", "40 XP earned once. Keep exploring at any time.": "9bf99bbdd767fe97", "Change an input in the experiment, then complete all three checks.": "76a5bcd6bf724304", "Complete all three checks correctly to unlock your stamp.": "a3b11c9045868bac", "Ready. Claim your evidence stamp for 40 XP.": "984db419a303162e", "Explore this experiment and complete its three knowledge checks first.": "253162a2a934cff5", "Frame the evidence question": "e19669bcb4cafcb2", "Pending decision": "d949379234cd611e", "Eligible population": "bcd32a90608c2f69", "Observable measure": "7492f199cdcbe7fe", "Decision horizon": "2f613ef3c4a17921", "\" type=\"text\" maxlength=\"180\" value=\"": "35ee5f49ff032980", "Build my question": "a60bfe9c771d9d36", "Name the decision and evidence before choosing software.": "fa0a946c4e84ce06", "What could evidence clarify?": "d2aa3603dd34ee4b", "Name the person making the decision.": "6f281f4cd1819379", "Choose a population and a period.": "aa25748c7aa7ed62", "Keep your question neutral about the result.": "ed1df56858390202", "Missing outcomes change the story": "9b684a9c5e08a571", "Fictional library deliveries: 20 eligible deliveries, 8 known late, and some with unknown outcomes. Missing outcomes are never silently treated as on time.": "c089c8370aa531c5", "Unknown outcomes:": "c80d775e9b965237", "Which denominator answers your question?": "b3a7963a310055a0", "Predict the late rate among known outcomes.": "278d757ff2f0886f", "Increase missingness. How wide are the all-delivery bounds?": "76b807eadf775397", "Describe both populations explicitly.": "dc58ab794ab784c7", "Five fictional delivery times: 18, 20, 22, 24, and a value you control. All values are minutes.": "dec31618c75f400d", "Purple: observations · blue: mean · teal: median. Axis fixed at 0–100 minutes.": "0ab4e94068eed22c", "An unusual observation can still be valid. Inspect its source before deciding to exclude it.": "3bb66459243058de", "Predict what happens before increasing the final time.": "c20b3095fb5fb61e", "Switch displays and change the bins.": "6a9c80b30b2ede44", "Explain why a distribution summary differs from an individual value.": "7757b5a734e5f25d", "Fictional quality screen for 1,000 products. Sensitivity is 80%; specificity is 80%. Change the base rate and watch positive screens split into true events and false alarms.": "5c0a3f2fc40cc6a2", "Sensitivity is P(positive | event). The event probability among positive screens is P(event | positive). Their denominators differ.": "e602f97328f571d7", "Does 80% sensitivity mean 80% of positives are true?": "2c77c5d56d10da22", "Start with a rare event.": "31aa945c4f3ce8fc", "Increase its base rate while keeping sensitivity fixed.": "2f6d0d5d39befcc7", "Find the denominator among positive screens.": "5aefba65a7dd57f7", "Second experiment: expected profit and downside": "668ffdc9b42ac2ee", "A fictional outdoor workshop chooses between a large setup and a flexible setup. Payoffs are net profit in hundreds of dollars. Large: 90 in dry weather, −40 in rain. Flexible: 45 dry, 10 rain.": "bc06695fe9ffea15", "An educational model, separate from the course lab: normal population, true mean 50 minutes, known population SD 12 minutes. Each line is one independent sample mean ± z × SE. Blue/teal intervals cover the true mean; amber intervals miss.": "1bc448b6df877578", "More observations improve precision under this model. They do not repair selection bias. Your course mean-estimation lab uses Student t when population SD is unknown.": "760e7bb9a6507c51", "Compare n = 25 with n = 100.": "734520e8828f2fa9", "Turn bias on, then increase n.": "81a53df2d20f6f33", "Distinguish a confidence interval from the spread of individual observations.": "14fce18efbcc8e8c", ", what does": "fa231a6b0b397bed", "tell us about whether to": "46b8890e72423734", "? State what data are available and what remains unknown.": "82679244788f7cbc", "Among observed outcomes": "5590d848a738f491", "All eligible deliveries: best/worst late-rate bounds": "d76bdbb1634c34dc", "8 known late / {known} observed outcomes. Across all 20 eligible deliveries, the rate lies between 8/20 and {upper}/20, depending on unknown outcomes.": "cf4503cb9165b0eb", "\" width=\"": "372526544af04c05", "\" height=\"": "4048ce3090aabbdb", "Count per {width}-minute bin": "4034efc6fe43b1f3", "22.0 min": "085bcf22f262ba96", "\" y=\"45\" width=\"": "0891e79f7d30866f", "Positive screens only: {positive} products": "2c2dd9cdba1d3608", "Teal: true event · amber: false alarm": "1037687b4bbe9745", "Large setup": "812b7122686d2d48", "Flexible setup": "d23b4fac7df79bf4", "Expected profit · hundreds of dollars": "33c14ea3aa05de7f", "Expected values tie at rain probability 47.4%. At {rain}%, {result}. The large setup still loses 40 if it rains; the flexible setup earns 10. Expected value is not a guarantee.": "d604ca93a1228d2d", "the expected values tie": "3f18912329d4d5b8", "the large setup has higher expected profit": "a4a2c56da2c3f5a2", "the flexible setup has higher expected profit": "366c3d17cb0ee74a", "Mean = 50 · 100 intervals · axis expands to fit": "9e8eb451590a48ba", "Seed {seed}. The sample-mean model is Normal(50 + {bias}, 12/√{sampleSize}). The {confidence}% known-SD interval uses z = {z}. One finite run need not match nominal coverage exactly. With bias, the stated unbiased procedure no longer has its intended coverage.": "c5422ab3a8f04568", "\" src=\"": "0c14ce14640c4f8d", "If the embedded viewer is unavailable,": "2c164fbba5c8e55c", "\">open the PDF separately": "d88337a3ec3fda21", "Gallery unavailable": "677af1a8e28d2e17", "\" alt=\"Session": "8c288258dd1cd930", "visual reference, page": "51f58fb7de3091b4", "at full size ↗": "5090267dcc87ba7f", "Use the full visual reference and its accessible reading link above.": "5f92bbd03a857f54", "{count} resources": "4d488c186c911d4c", "Try another search or category": "662ae4102c3758e4", "Read evidence passport": "4ae5166a940656ba", "Read device-local practice progress and the visible experiment configuration.": "ac05dcc0bb007d1a", "Configure practice experiment": "7d6d229eabe82521", "Select a released experiment and adjust its visible numeric controls. This records exploration but does not complete a knowledge check or stamp.": "490513dc5516622c", "Only released sessions 1–5 are available.": "f4fb96eef1b6ee90", "Invalid control value for this experiment.": "38dd291b5b0ed807", "Submit practice answer": "116c329ac184d6ac", "Submit one optional knowledge-check choice and receive the same explanation and device-local XP as the visible buttons. Choices are indexed 0–2.": "daf9b2d7ddccf5f1", "Choose a valid question and answer.": "36ba83809b17ca59", "Claim evidence stamp": "cefc92af257626f9", "Complete a device-local practice stamp after the experiment has been explored and all three knowledge checks answered correctly. This has no course-grade effect.": "e017a9bceb8892a5"}